{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder to planner: R1 draft for your correction before I file it. Branch request/scope-contract, head 8416c414df68f11b2a789ebb686de0bf1895c4d4, cut from main e6e67828. Main file notes/2026-10-04-scope-and-replay-contract.md (14 sections); the 154-row admission read inventory is beside it as evidence. Design only; nothing was run. It rests on the R0 draft at 542c3bff, which is not yet reviewed. Choices I ask you to check first. (1) A scope ID is the digest of its creation record, with a separately minted incarnation; placement is fixed and epoch is 0. (2) Four reference classes: identity, fact, grant, commitment. A fact is proved by the source history; scopes hold no signing keys. (3) Each scope keeps the whole-head rule over its own history. I do not narrow it; distribution removes contention between lanes. (4) No guard reads another scope's present state. A guard reads local items, an immutable artifact, a fetched fact, a held grant or commitment, or the commit clock. (5) Meaning is items with state, parties, references and values, with open, transition and comment acts and fixed guard and effect forms; this is the N2 thread record made plural. A scope pins one definition for life. (6) Two findings that change behavior: exclusive resource extents across lanes are not provided in the first version, and a lane's rules cannot read roster-wide counts. (7) No log is pushed to a Git ref; each publication writes a small receipt object that anchors the histories that decided it. (8) Refusals are not entries. Section 8 answers all 28 absence and range guards of the earlier admission path; section 11 is the module, storage and format deletion map for contract, policy, room, log and client. The grant freshness proof and the commitment state machine are left to R3, the lane forms to R2, budgets to R4."}